51645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,64,4779,400,65,4779,400,66,4779,323,400,67,3,387,6785,1931,5109,1741,429,400,18,21,64,488,220,19,65,488,220,19,66,488,220,18,67,284,220,17,20,2418,220,7379,279,7192,897,315,198,78045,64,1124,15136,1124,26888,90,65,92,1124,15136,1124,26888,58,18,15370,66,92,1124,15136,1124,26888,58,19,15370,67,92,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $d$ be positive real numbers such that $36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25.$  Find the maximum value of\n\\[a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2de17349d2d1c86b044b03c314cdb17fa7aaf2bf8b169f36bc351ecf136830e7"},"seconds":103.44844484329224,"seed":1146583309391590530,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the maximum value of \\(a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}\\) given the constraint \\(36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25\\), we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or apply the AM-GM inequality directly.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the expression we want to maximize in a form suitable for applying the AM-GM inequality:\n\\[\na \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}.\n\\]\n\nTo apply the AM-GM inequality, we need to express the terms in a way that fits the constraint \\(36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25\\). We can rewrite the constraint in a form that will help us apply the inequality:\n\n\\[\n36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25.\n\\]\n\nWe can use the AM-GM inequality on the terms \\(36a, 4b, 4c, 3d\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36a + 4b + 4c + 3d}{4} \\geq \\sqrt[4]{36a \\cdot 4b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 3d}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the constraint \\(36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25\\) into the inequality, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{4} \\geq \\sqrt[4]{36a \\cdot 4b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 3d}.\n\\]\n\nRaising both sides to the fourth power, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)^4 \\geq 36a \\cdot 4b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 3d.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{390625}{256} \\geq 36a \\cdot 4b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 3d.\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{390625}{256} \\geq 1728abcd.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\na \\cdot b \\cdot c \\cdot d \\leq \\frac{390625}{256 \\cdot 1728} = \\frac{390625}{442368}.\n\\]\n\nHowever, we need to find the maximum value of \\(a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}\\). To achieve equality in the AM-GM inequality, we need:\n\n\\[\n36a = 4b = 4c = 3d.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(36a = 4b = 4c = 3d = k\\). Then we have:\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{k}{36}, \\quad b = \\frac{k}{4}, \\quad c = \\frac{k}{4}, \\quad d = \\frac{k}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the constraint, we get:\n\n\\[\nk + k + k + k = 4k = 25 \\implies k = \\frac{25}{4}.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{25}{4 \\cdot 36} = \\frac{25}{144}, \\quad b = \\frac{25}{4 \\cdot 4} = \\frac{25}{16}, \\quad c = \\frac{25}{16}, \\quad d = \\frac{25}{4 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{25}{12}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the expression \\(a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}\\):\n\n\\[\na \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d} = \\frac{25}{144} \\times \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{16}} \\times \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{25}{16}} \\times \\sqrt[4]{\\frac{25}{12}}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each term, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{144} \\times \\frac{5}{4} \\times \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{25}{16}} \\times \\sqrt[4]{\\frac{25}{12}} = \\frac{25}{144} \\times \\frac{5}{4} \\times \\frac{5^{1/3}}{2^{4/3}} \\times \\frac{5^{1/4}}{12^{1/4}} = \\frac{25 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5^{1/3} \\cdot 5^{1/4}}{144 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2^{4/3} \\cdot 12^{1/4}} = \\frac{5^3 \\cdot 5^{1/3} \\cdot 5^{1/4}}{144 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2^{4/3} \\cdot 12^{1/4}}.\n\\]\n\nCombining the exponents of 5, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5^{1 + 1/3 + 1/4}}{144 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2^{4/3} \\cdot 12^{1/4}} = \\frac{5^{19/12}}{144 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2^{4/3} \\cdot 12^{1/4}}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the denominator, we find:\n\n\\[\n144 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2^{4/3} \\cdot 12^{1/4} = 576 \\cdot 2^{4/3} \\cdot 12^{1/4} = 576 \\cdot 2^{4/3} \\cdot 2^{1/4} \\cdot 3^{1/4} = 576 \\cdot 2^{19/12} \\cdot 3^{1/4}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5^{19/12}}{576 \\cdot 2^{19/12} \\cdot 3^{1/4}} = \\frac{1}{576} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{19/12} \\cdot 3^{-1/4} = \\frac{1}{576} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{19/12} \\cdot 3^{-1/4} = \\frac{1}{144} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{7/12} \\cdot 3^{-1/4}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the maximum value is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{25}{144}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{25}{144}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
